From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 17:19:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: blend with Metal's own state where Metal can
 express it

KosmicKrisp lowered every blend into the fragment shader, which made the
shader's MSL a function of the colour-blend state.  That state belongs to the
fragment-output interface library, not to the library holding the fragment
shader, so the shader could not be compiled until the two were linked, and
VK_EXT_graphics_pipeline_library's link had to carry Metal's whole MSL to AIR
compile - about 95% of its cost, and the reason
dEQP-VK.pipeline.pipeline_library.graphics_library.misc.other.compare_link_times
warns.

Metal has a fixed-function blend for every Vulkan blend factor and every
Vulkan blend operation, including the constant-colour factors and the
saturate, plus the write mask, and it applies them in the attachment's own
format, so an sRGB target blends in linear space exactly as the specification
asks.  Use it.  What Metal has no form of at all - a logic op, an advanced
blend operation - keeps kk_lower_fs_blend, and so does a dual-source factor
until the MSL backend can emit a second colour index, and so does a pipeline
that makes blend enables, equations, write masks, the logic op or the
attachment count dynamic, because Metal bakes all of those into the pipeline
state.  kk_blend_is_metal_expressible is the single predicate, and it
deliberately ignores MESA_VK_DYNAMIC_CB_BLEND_ADVANCED: the runtime sets that
bit whenever advancedBlendCoherentOperations is disabled, which means the
advanced state is ignored rather than deferred.

Blend constants stay dynamic on both paths.  Metal takes them on the encoder,
so a pipeline blending with Metal's state binds them there instead of writing
them into the root descriptor table, and the bind repeats when the shader is
rebound because the pipeline can change without the constants changing.

The attachment's shape decides whether Metal can own the equation at all, in
two ways the lowered path used to hide.  KosmicKrisp declared the fragment
function's return with the attachment's component count, which was safe only
because kk_lower_fs_blend narrowed every colour store to match; with the blend
left to Metal, that return both names components the declared type lacks - the
MSL failed to compile for every attachment narrower than four components - and
would rob the blend unit of the source alpha it weights with, since Metal reads
that alpha from the return value.  A shader left independent of the blend state
therefore keeps returning four components, which Metal narrows to the
attachment itself while still supplying the destination alpha of 1.0 that a
format without one is defined to read.  And a format Metal has no native form
of is reached through the view's MTLTextureSwizzleChannels, which sits between
the shader and the texture but not between the texture and the blend unit, so
the three four-bit packed formats blend their destination in the hardware's
channel order; those stay on the lowered path, which reads the destination back
through the same swizzle.

A fragment shader may also declare an index 1 output that no blend factor
names.  It feeds nothing but a dual-source factor, so it is dead - but
nir_lower_blend is what consumed it, unconditionally, and Metal has neither a
second colour source nor, in this backend, a second colour index to emit.
Leaving it in place while Metal blends produced a shader the backend could not
express; dropping it keeps the pipeline on the fixed-function path and leaves
the same MSL whichever expressible blend state the link supplies.  A factor
that does name SRC1 still falls back to kk_lower_fs_blend.

The new kosmickrisp_metal_blend test renders a covered target through fourteen
blend states - every operation, the dst, one-minus-dst, constant and saturate
factors, three write masks - and compares every channel the format holds
against the same equation evaluated on the host.  It runs them against an
attachment of each shape: R8G8B8A8_UNORM and R8G8B8A8_SRGB, the three-, two-
and one-component R5G6B5_UNORM_PACK16, R8G8_UNORM and R8_UNORM, and the
swizzle-emulated B4G4R4A4_UNORM_PACK16, plus an R8G8B8A8_UNORM target drawn
with a fragment shader carrying a dead index 1 output.  It passes on both
paths, which is what MESA_KK_DEBUG=no_metal_blend is for:
it forces the lowered path so the two can be compared, and so a suspected
fixed-function blend defect can be bisected without a rebuild.
A one-off benchmark timed four hundred covering draws over a 1024x1024
attachment with a GPU timestamp around the pass; on an M1 Ultra with the host
otherwise busy, the best of four alternating runs was 8.57 us per draw with
Metal's blend and 9.54 us with the lowered one, so the fixed-function path is
no slower and is the better default for a monolithic pipeline as well.
